Source package: mutter

tap-key.patch
    Lets a modifier tapped on its own send a key combination, configured with tap-key and
    tap-key-sends in org.gnome.mutter. Mutter already gives a lone tap of a modifier a meaning for
    two keys, the overlay key and the locate-pointer key, and this adds a third one, which sends the
    key combination tap-key-sends names. Held together with other keys it stays an ordinary
    modifier.

    The combination goes through a virtual keyboard, so it reaches keybindings and global shortcuts
    the same way a key pressed by hand does. configure-gnome-keys.sh has the right Super send
    XF86Launch9, which no physical key sends, for Claude Desktop's Quick Entry shortcut.

    The test is added to src/tests/keybindings.c, which runs during the build. It holds the tap key
    together with another key, which must send nothing, and then taps it on its own, which must
    trigger a keybinding bound to the combination it sends.

    The tap is detected by process_special_modifier_key in src/core/keybindings.c, shared by all
    three keys, and the settings are read in src/core/prefs.c next to the other two. After a version
    bump, check that both still handle the overlay and locate-pointer keys this way, since the patch
    follows them.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      tap the right Super on its own     should send XF86Launch9, which opens Claude Quick Entry
      right Super with another key       should act as Super, for example Ctrl+Super+L locks

xdg-session-management.patch
    Backport of https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4806 from mutter 51. Mutter
    50 has the session management protocol only under its draft name xx_session_manager_v1, and
    offers it only when gnome-shell runs with MUTTER_DEBUG_SESSION_MANAGEMENT_PROTOCOL=1. The patch
    renames it to the final xdg_session_manager_v1 and offers it to every client. A client which
    names its windows then has mutter restore their workspace, monitor, position, size and state.

    It is for Firefox, which binds only xdg_session_manager_v1 since version 154. Firefox restores
    its windows with it only together with the patches in patches/firefox/, which fix Mozilla bug
    2064100 and enable the protocol on GNOME again, and the one in patches/gtk+3.0/, which adds the
    xdg-toplevel-realized signal Firefox needs. Without them, MOZ_LOG=WidgetWayland:4 shows which
    check in IsSessionRestoreSupported (Firefox's widget/gtk/nsAppShell.cpp) disabled it.

    Firefox sends get_session at every start, even where it does not register its windows. Mutter
    50.1 and 51.1 crash when a client sends get_session after the session manager could not be
    created, which happens when ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/session.gvdb cannot be read (mutter
    issue 5168, open). This patch makes that crash reachable. If gnome-shell ever crashes when
    Firefox starts, look in the journal for "Could not create session manager" and move that file
    away.

    session.gvdb only grows, by about 270 bytes for every window Firefox ever had. Firefox never
    removes a window from its session, and mutter never prunes stored windows on its own. Mutter
    also cannot remove a window stored in an earlier run of a session, so a client could not clean
    up even if it tried (mutter issue 5174, open, with patches). Moving the file away resets all
    stored window places, of every application.

    The patch is the four commits of the merge request. Only the test change of the last one
    differs: upstream it edits src/tests/wayland-xdg-session-management-tests.c, which was split
    out of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c after 50.1, so here the same line is removed from
    wayland-unit-tests.c. The existing session management tests in wayland-unit-tests.c run during
    the build against the renamed protocol.

    Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships mutter 51 or newer. Before that, after a version bump, check
    that src/meson.build still has no xdg-session-management entry of its own, and note that with
    wayland-protocols 1.48 or newer the build uses the protocol XML from wayland-protocols instead of
    the copy in src/wayland/protocol/.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with wayland-info from the wayland-utils package:

      wayland-info | grep session        should list xdg_session_manager_v1, and xx_session_manager_v1
                                         from xx-session-management.patch

xx-session-management.patch
    Offers the draft protocol, xx_session_manager_v1, again next to the final one, for Chromium.
    Chromium speaks only the draft (up to at least 156, see crbug.com/409099413), so with
    xdg-session-management.patch alone it found no session manager. Both protocols are served by the
    same code and share the stored sessions, and each session records which one it was created
    through. For the draft, add_toplevel refuses only a name in use by a toplevel of the same
    session object, as in mutter 50.1, which Chromium was written against. Mutter 50.1 offered the
    draft only with MUTTER_DEBUG_SESSION_MANAGEMENT_PROTOCOL=1, this offers it to every client.

    Chromium uses it only with its WaylandSessionManagement feature, enabled in
    chrome://flags/#wayland-session-management or with --enable-features=WaylandSessionManagement,
    which chromium.d/ of this repository adds. Without the feature, Chromium and the Electron
    applications built on it bind the global but send nothing, checked with Chromium, Signal and
    Slack. Chromium names each window anew at every start: it restores a window under its previous
    name, then removes that name and adds the window under a new one, so unlike Firefox it does not
    make session.gvdb grow. As with Firefox, the first start after enabling it shows windows
    unplaced, and from the next start on they return to their places.

    The session management tests in wayland-unit-tests.c run during the build against the final
    protocol, which the patch leaves as it is. The draft is tested with Chromium itself by
    tests/run-chromium-session.sh, see tests/README.

    Mutter 51 drops the draft, so when Ubuntu ships it, this patch has to be ported to mutter 51's
    own code. Drop it once Chromium speaks xdg_session_manager_v1.

xdg-toplevel-session-rename.patch
    Implements the rename request of xdg_toplevel_session_v1, which mutter leaves unimplemented,
    also in 51.1 and on main. libwayland-server aborts a compositor which receives a request without
    a handler, so any client sending rename ended GNOME Shell and with it the session.
    xdg-session-management.patch offers the protocol to every client, so the two go together. A name
    already known to the session raises name_in_use, as add_toplevel does, and otherwise the state
    stored under the old name moves to the new one.

    Neither Firefox nor Chromium sends rename. tests/run-rename-check.sh checks it with a minimal
    client, see tests/README. The crash is reported as mutter issue 5177. Drop the patch once mutter
    implements rename.

save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch
    Mutter saves where a window of a session was only when the window is unmanaged, and only while
    the session object still exists, while the protocol says destroying a session preserves the
    current state of its toplevels. When a client quits without destroying its windows first,
    libwayland destroys its objects in the order of their IDs, so a window whose surface, toplevel
    and xdg_wm_base all have higher IDs than the session is unmanaged only once the session is gone,
    and its place is lost. GTK 4 binds xdg_wm_base and creates every window but the first after its
    session, and quits without destroying its windows, so Text Editor, quitting with several windows
    open, had only its first window placed again at the next start. With this patch mutter saves the
    state of every toplevel still having a window when the session is destroyed, by the client or by
    its disconnect, unless the client removed the session. The same code serves
    xdg_session_manager_v1 and the draft from xx-session-management.patch.

    The test xdg-session-management-restore of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c is run a second time
    with the client destroying its session while its toplevel is still mapped
    (restore-session-destroyed), which fails without the change. The tests run during the build.
    The fix is proposed upstream as mutter merge request 5453. Drop the patch once mutter saves a
    session's toplevels when the session is destroyed.

save-toplevels-on-change.patch
    Mutter stores where a window of a session is only when the window is unmanaged, and with the
    patch above when the session is destroyed, while the protocol says the compositor updates the
    state of a toplevel whenever it changes. When GNOME Shell crashes, or the machine resets or
    loses power, no window is unmanaged, so every window comes back where it was the last time it
    was stored, possibly days earlier, or opens unplaced. With this patch mutter stores the state
    of a toplevel whenever its window moves, changes its size, its maximized, fullscreen or
    minimized state, or its workspace, and writes session.gvdb 3 seconds after the first change,
    as it did for a window unmanaged. Windows without a workspace, not placed yet or shown on all
    workspaces, are left to the store at unmanage, as is a toplevel the client removed. A restored
    toplevel is stored on change only once its stored state has been applied, which would
    otherwise be replaced while it is read.

    The test restore-changed of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c has a second client restore the
    session while the first one still has its moved window mapped, and fails without the change,
    with the window placed anew. The tests run during the build. With Ptyxis it is also tested by
    patches/ptyxis/tests/run-ptyxis-session.sh, which ends mutter with SIGKILL and starts it
    again, and whose second run restores no window without this patch. Reported upstream as
    mutter issue 5181 and proposed as mutter merge request 5455. Drop the patch once mutter stores
    a toplevel's state when it changes.

wayland-keyboard-test-sync-point.patch
    Upstream's commit c6a16523f, which fixes a race in the keyboard tests of the build's test suite.
    A test waited for the test client's window and then for its sync point, and waiting for the
    window could dispatch the sync point request, so the test hung until meson killed it after 360
    seconds and failed the build. It changes only the test. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships mutter
    51, which has it.
